v5.5.0: the status pill stops calling a silent detector a backend failure

The red dot was read as "the site cannot reach its backend". It never meant that. It meant the
newest reading in this profile was over an hour old — which is exactly what happens when the
recording computer is switched off, and it sent a morning to the Firebase console looking for a
quota problem that was never there.

Three defects, all in how the pill decides what to say:

1. It never distinguished the database connection from detector freshness. Both arrived as one
   red dot, and on a phone the CSS hides the label, so the colour was the entire message. The
   SDK already publishes the real answer at `.info/connected`; that now drives "Offline", and
   data age gets its own wording plus a title that says the connection is fine.

2. It only recomputed inside the `latest` value callback, so with no new data there was no
   callback and the badge froze. A dashboard left open while the detector died kept showing a
   green LIVE indefinitely; one that had rendered "3h ago" stayed red even after data resumed.
   The pill is now derived from state on a one-second tick.

3. It skipped the update entirely when `latest` was absent (`if (_latestData)`), so a profile
   that had never received a reading kept whatever green the profile loader had just set. No
   data at all looked healthier than data one hour old. Absence is now a reported state.

Data between two minutes and an hour old was previously shown as "connected" green; it is now
amber. The platform is working and the detector is quiet — that is neither success nor failure,
and it was the one state the two-colour pill could not express.

tools/check-status-rule.mjs exercises the shipped file's rule across all ten cases, including the
reported symptom and a future-dated timestamp. It lives outside public/, so it is not served.
